   "CHARACTER SHEET - WallPieces   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these two houses)\n\nThe two houses as cut paper, no animals, laid on the plain GROUND #5B6E7E sheet.\n🔴 NO PIECE OVERLAPS ANOTHER PIECE ANYWHERE ON THIS SHEET.\n\n1 🔴 ONE HOUSE, TAKEN APART: at most six pieces laid slightly separated so each is readable - the\n  plaster panel in OUR PLASTER #F1E9D8 with a visible grain and 0 marks inside it · a roof piece ·\n  TWO TIMBER #4A3323 strips down its two sides · a TIMBER door · a sill. 🔴 THE FRAMING IS THESE\n  STRIPS, NOT A DRAWN BAND - there is no line anywhere on the plaster. Beside it the same house in\n  THEIR PLASTER #DFB6A9.\n2 🔴 TWO POSTS MEETING: the two houses' inner timber strips set side by side so their edges touch\n  and the sheet shows between them as ONE HAIRLINE - 🔴 at thumbnail size that reads as a single\n  dark post. Then the same two pulled apart to a FINGER width, and again to a PALM width. 🔴 Same\n  thing, three widths - fix that here, it is the whole book.\n3 🔴 THE LEAN PLATE, four states at one size, both houses full height, seen straight on:\n  A · LEFT +6, RIGHT 0 - the left house tipped right so its top corner rests on the right house,\n    the posts touching, GROUND between them a HAIRLINE. 🔴 Its sill tips right too.\n  B · LEFT +4, RIGHT -1 - a FINGER of ground between the posts, opening from the top down.\n  C · LEFT 0 upright, RIGHT -4 - a PALM of ground, the widest in the book; 🔴 the right house's\n    sill now tips LEFT.\n  D · 🔴 LEFT +3, RIGHT -3 - both tipped toward each other so their top corners meet, posts\n    touching, ground a HAIRLINE again, 🔴 BOTH SILLS TIPPING INWARD. Set A and D side by side: one\n    is one-sided and one is even, and that is the landing.\n4 🔴 THE WINDOW BOX, large: one TIMBER box on a sill with at most 3 GERANIUM #C8532E petal pieces\n  and 0 leaves - shown upright and shown tipped with its house. The only saturated piece.\n5 THE DOORS, three plates: shut flush in its frame · standing open with TIMBER INNER #2F2118 as\n  the inside face (🔴 a different face, not a shadow) · half open with a paw hooked on the edge.\n6 🔴 THE EVENING SET: every piece above cut again ONE STEP DARKER AND WARMER, so a whole scene can\n  be swapped at once - 🔴 in that set OUR PLASTER and THEIR PLASTER become THE SAME orange paper.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: WallPieces.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - CatKit   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nCut paper, same make as the houses - flat pieces with scissor edges, no outline, no shading, no\nsheen, set edge to edge. It must look made of the same material as everything else.\n\nBODY: a kitten in CAT #C0A98A, the body one flat piece with a long thin tail cut as its own piece,\n  short limbs as separate strips, two triangle ears, a flat nose and three fine cut whiskers a\n  side. 🔴 NO CLOTHES, NO COLLAR. Bristled fur = the outline cut into a row of small points, 🔴\n  never drawn strands and never a fuzzy edge.\nEYES: two small dark cut discs, no eyebrow, no expression, no catchlight. 🔴 The acting is the\n  spread of the legs, the line of the back and the height of the tail.\n🔴 THE POSTURE LADDER IS THE ACTING - seven at one size in a row: CROUCHED (squatting at a sill,\n  one forepaw stretched out and just let go, head following something to the side) · STARTLED\n  (half turned, both forepaws up at the chest, shoulders pulled in, 🔴 THE TAIL CUT AS A FAT\n  BOTTLE-BRUSH SHAPE, ears back) · LEANING OVER A SILL (both forepaws planted on it, body pushed\n  forward) · 🔴 BACK-PUSH (spine and the back of the head flat against a wall, both hind feet dug\n  into gravel, knees bent hard, eyes shut, mouth wide, tail stiff out behind) · 🔴 BACK-PUSH LOW\n  (the same but crouched further down, forehead also against the wall, hind legs straight out,\n  teeth set) · FLAT OUT (on the stomach on a sill, nose almost touching something small, both\n  forepaws either side) · UP ON TWO with both forearms thrown straight overhead, heels lifted,\n  tail bolt upright · 🔴 SHOULDER-PUSH (facing the wall, both palms flat on it, one shoulder set\n  against it, one hind leg stretched far back - 🔴 THE MIRROR OF BACK-PUSH, and the book turns on\n  that reversal) · SITTING with the tail curled round the front feet, head down.\n🔴 THE TWO FOREPAWS, LARGE: both palms flat on a plaster panel, and one paw laid flat on a sill.\nPlain ground, no scenery. 0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: CatKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - BadgerKit   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\nCut paper, same make as CatKit - attach the approved CatKit sheet so the two are made by one hand\nand the size between them is settled. Plain GROUND. Colour: BADGER #6F6A62.\n\nBUILD: a young badger, low and wide: a heavy wedge body, short thick limbs, a stubby tail, small\n  round ears. 🔴 THE FACE STRIPE IS ONE CUT PIECE of #F1E9D8 laid into the head shape with two\n  dark bands either side of it. 🔴 AT THUMBNAIL SIZE HE IS A LOW WIDE WEDGE AND THE CAT IS A TALL\n  THIN CURVE - never confuse them. No clothes.\nFACE: two small dark cut discs, a level mouth. 🔴 HIS FACE DOES NOT CHANGE FOR FOURTEEN PAGES.\n🔴 THE LADDER, five at one size: STOPPING A ROLLING THING (one forepaw pressed down on it against\n  the gravel, head turned up and to one side) · LOOKING STRAIGHT UP (neck thrown right back, both\n  forepaws together at the belly) · REACHING (flat on the stomach, both forearms stretched out to\n  one side, hind feet braced on his own sill) · 🔴 HOLDING A DOOR (both palms flat on the door\n  face, heels dug into gravel, the whole body slanted back away from it, mouth open) · 🔴\n  SHOULDER-PUSH, the mirror of the cat's - facing his own wall, both palms on it, one shoulder\n  set, one hind leg stretched back, eyes shut · SITTING with the front feet together, head down.\n🔴 He never points at a wall, never explains and never takes anything out of the cat's hands.\nPlain ground, no scenery. 0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: BadgerKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - AlleySet   (bake FOURTH, attach as @image4)\n\nThe place, empty of animals, in cut paper on the GROUND #5B6E7E sheet, no piece overlapping any\nother. 🔴 Attach the approved WallPieces sheet - the houses here are those pieces, nothing new.\n\nTHE PLACE, fixed for fourteen pages: a narrow cobbled lane seen straight on, 🔴 ONLY THE LOWER\n  TWO STOREYS OF THE TWO HOUSES AND THE TWO DOORWAYS, the roofs cut off by the top of the frame on\n  most pages. A GRAVEL #9A8C77 band across the bottom with at most 24 cut stones in it and 0\n  beyond. Two TIMBER sills, one per house, set at the same height. 🔴 Draw the lane once, wide,\n  straight on, from the middle of it.\n🔴 THE TWO LOOSE THINGS - the evidence of this book: ONE ACORN, a small two-piece cut shape, and\n  ONE GLASS MARBLE, a single small disc of GROUND #5B6E7E paper 🔴 (it is the same sheet as the sky\n  - the only round piece that is). Plates: the acorn at the LEFT end of a sill · at the RIGHT end\n  of the same sill, 🔴 with the left end plainly empty · dead centre on a level sill · the marble\n  at the right sill and again out on the gravel to its left · 🔴 THE TWO OF THEM TOUCHING, side by\n  side on the gravel exactly between the two doorways.\nALSO: a shutter shut and the same shutter swung open (TIMBER INNER inside face) · a wisp of straw\n  caught on an eave, and the same wisp slid to the eave's end · at most 9 plaster flecks as tiny\n  cut pieces · two pairs of scuffed heel marks cut into the gravel.\n🔴 THE EVENING LANE: the whole set cut again in the WallPieces EVENING SET, 🔴 both plaster panels\n  now the same orange paper, the sky one step deeper.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals, no signs. SCENE token: AlleySet."
