   "CHARACTER SHEET - FeatherFour   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these four)\n\nSame make as the book: laid on warm grey paper #DCD8D0. These four are the ONLY objects in the\nbook painted with a fine sable at botanical-plate precision - shaft, individual barbs, the split\nat the tip. Lay them side by side on plain paper AT CORRECT RELATIVE SCALE.\n\n1 STRIPED: the longest of the four, slim, warm brown #6B4A2C with bold cross-bars running the\n  whole length, a pale shaft, a slightly ragged tip. Reads as stripes at thumbnail size.\n2 SPECKLED: shorter and broader, chestnut #7A5236 scattered with round dark spots of two sizes,\n  spots crowding near the tip. Reads as spots at thumbnail size.\n3 LARGE GREY: the biggest by far, wide and stiff, grey #8B8D8A, a strong dark shaft and long\n  fine barbs combed to one side. It is about as long as the young sandpiper's whole body.\n4 THE SMALL ONE: a soft warm white #F4F1E9 down feather, ABOUT THE SIZE OF THE BIRD'S TOE - the\n  smallest by a wide margin. NO PATTERN AT ALL: no bar, no spot, no dark shaft. Fluffy, loose,\n  slightly curled. It is still painted with the same fine sable and the same care - it is plain\n  because there is nothing to paint, NOT because it was painted roughly.\nSCALE PLATE: all four laid in a row beside a young sandpiper's foot AT THE SAME DEPTH - the\n  large grey one as long as the bird's body, the white one as long as one toe. Perspective must\n  never flip these sizes on any page.\nWET STATE for 1: the same striped feather with water running off the shaft and the barbs stuck\n  together in points. MUDDY STATE for 2: the lower third stained near-black with mud.\nPlain paper, no scenery, no bird. No text or numerals. SCENE token: FeatherFour.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - SandpiperYoung   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nSame medium and paper. A flat shape with a soft brush edge and at most one interior mark - do\nnot render it finely just because it is alone on the sheet. The feathers outrank it in finish.\n\nBODY: a young wader, buff #A8987F, paler belly, a darker flat patch across the back, short\n  stubby wings that never look flight-ready. Legs are two thin dark strokes; the beak is a\n  single long dark taper, straight and blunt-tipped. Neck extends and folds right down into the\n  body. It is small: the large grey feather is as long as its whole body.\nFACE: one round dark eye with a SEPARATE short brow mark above it - the brow is the acting and\n  must lift, pull in and tilt. No dot-eye without a brow, no catchlight, no drawn feathers on\n  the head.\nFIXED GRADE: two legs, beak used as a hand. THE FEET NEVER LEAVE THE GROUND IN ANY VIEW ON THIS\n  SHEET - even the flapping pose keeps both feet planted in the sand. No clothes, no fingers.\nSHEET: side view standing, neck stretched, beak forward / three-quarter running with the neck\n  low and both feet on the ground / braced backwards pulling something with the beak, tail down,\n  toes dug in / one wing HALF raised sideways showing the plain pale down underneath, head\n  turned to look under it / sitting tucked down with the neck buried and only the beak showing;\n  plus two close-ups - alarmed (brow high, eye wide) and quietly pleased (eye a crescent).\nPlain paper, no scenery. SCENE token: SandpiperYoung.",
