   "CHARACTER SHEET - CalfSpot   (bake FIRST, before any scene)\n\nFACE: a young black-and-white spotted calf, short blunt muzzle, wide-set eyes. The head is bare\n  paper with two or three ink patches scribbled solid, one of them across an eye. The eye is a\n  drawn ring with a dark centre and a SEPARATE ink brow above it that lifts, knots and tilts.\n  Nostrils 2 short strokes, ear insides 2 strokes each.\nBODY: small, leggy, knock-kneed, four heads long, soft dewlap, small horn buds not yet through.\n  Patches sit as whole shapes, never as speckle; count them - 6 patches on the body, no more, and\n  they keep the same shape and place on every page so the calf is recognisable from behind.\n🔴 THE TAIL LADDER (draw both, same pose, same size, side by side): DOWN - hanging loose and\n  slightly curved, the tuft brushing the hock. UP - the whole tail thrown STRAIGHT UP above the\n  back, stiff as a pole, drawn with ONE overrun stroke that shoots past the tuft. This one mark\n  is the evidence of the whole book and must be the strongest thing on any page it appears on.\n🔴 GREEN SCRAPS: shreds of clover-green fill stuck to the muzzle, the lip corner and between two\n  front hooves - the same green as the trough, 5 to 8 scraps, present on every page from 1 to 14.\nSHEET: standing on four legs with the head buried in a trough / head flung up, ears pricked, tail\n  up / neck stretched right out with the muzzle shoved forward to point / body twisted round to\n  look at its own rump; plus three close-ups - eyes huge and mouth open in shock, eyes screwed\n  shut and muzzle wrinkled in a sulk, mouth open laughing with the eyes in half-moons. Plain\n  paper, no byre. SCENE token: CalfSpot.",
