   "CHARACTER SHEET - WolfCub   (bake this FIRST, attach as @image1)\n\nMedium: STYLE ANCHOR changjak-twobeams - two flat opaque plates on a dark board, hard cut edges,\n0 shading. Do NOT render this animal roundly or smoothly just because there is no scene behind him.\n\nBUILD\nA wolf CUB: head, ears and paws big, legs short, body low. He is ONE flat bare-board #151A20\nshape; night blue #2A4A72 lands only on his upper surfaces - top of the head, shoulder line, top\nof the muzzle, upper edge of each ear. Fur = 0 hairs, 0 tufts. Snow on him = flat reserve #F2EFE6\ncaps, at most 3 on the whole body.\n\nFACE\nEye = one small notch of BARE BOARD cut inside the lit shape; no white, no catchlight, no brow,\nno blush. Mouth = one cut line; calling opens it into a flat dark shape with 0 teeth, 0 tongue.\nNose pad and inner ear = one flat shape each. 🔴 0 claws in every view.\nDraw the head in FIVE STATES: mouth pursed round, blowing / mouth open calling, head tipped back /\neyes shut, forehead pressed flat to a surface / eyes wide, ears straight up / eyes half shut,\nears easy.\n\nCLOTHES: none. No clothes, shoes, collar, scarf, nothing carried, on any page.\n\n🔴 GRADE - FOUR LEGS EXCEPT WHEN HE STRIKES THE DOOR. POSTURE STRIP, seven flat figures:\n  1. standing four-square, one forepaw lifted clear, muzzle blowing;\n  2. up on the hind legs, ONE whole forepaw pressed flat and wide on a vertical surface, other\n     forepaw braced low on a stone, head tipped back;\n  3. 🔴 up on the hind legs, BOTH forepaws slammed flat side by side, arms straight, shoulders to\n     the ears, hind toes on a stone edge, body leaning in;\n  4. forehead pressed to a vertical surface, one slack forepaw below it, shoulders dropped, eyes\n     shut;\n  5. up on the hind legs, ONE ear pressed flat and spread against a vertical surface, cheek and\n     nose touching it, both forepaws holding on, eyes squeezed shut;\n  6. 🔴 crouched low, one forepaw laid on a vertical surface and dragged straight DOWN, back\n     rounded, face brought near the ground;\n  7. flat on the belly, both forelegs stretched forward, hind legs long behind, muzzle pushed up\n     against a low surface.\nPlus ONE separate part: a forepaw alone, pad spread wide, toes splayed, seen pad-on.\n\nSILHOUETTE\nThe LONG-LEGGED SHAPE WITH TWO BIG POINTED EARS. He never shares a frame with the piglet, so the\ntest is against the door: his ear triangles must read against the plank edges at any size.\n\nREFERENCE SHEET\nOne flat dark field, figures floating, no ground, no scenery, no shadow, no lettering: full body\nside / three-quarter front / the seven-pose strip / the five head states / the forepaw part.\nSCENE token: WolfCub.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - PigletIn   (bake this SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nMedium: STYLE ANCHOR changjak-twobeams - two flat opaque plates on a dark board, hard cut edges,\n0 shading.\n\nBUILD\nA piglet, small and round: low heavy body, short legs, blunt snout, two floppy triangular ears,\nshort tail. He is ONE flat bare-board #151A20 shape and gap orange #E08A3C lands only on his\nupper surfaces - top of the back, top of the snout, upper edge of each ear, top of a foretrotter.\n0 hairs, 0 bristles.\n\nFACE\nEye = one small notch of BARE BOARD cut inside the lit shape; no white, no catchlight, no brow,\nno blush, no lashes. Mouth = one cut line; shouting = one flat dark shape, 0 teeth. Snout disc,\ninner ear and trotter = one flat shape each.\n🔴 HE IS NEVER DRAWN AFRAID - eyes stay round and wide, the brow area is never creased, the mouth\ncorners never pull down. He is COUNTING, not cowering.\nDraw the head in FOUR STATES: eyes wide, one ear turned aside / eyes wide above a quilt edge,\nmouth hidden / mouth wide open shouting, both ears up / eyes wide, cheek pressed flat to a surface.\n\nCLOTHES: none. No clothes, shoes or cap. The quilt is the only thing that covers him and it is\nnever worn, only pulled.\n\n🔴 GRADE - FOUR LEGS ON EVERY PAGE. POSTURE STRIP, five flat figures:\n  1. lying on his side, body completely still, ONE ear turned back, both foretrotters out in\n     front side by side;\n  2. lying, both foretrotters gripping a quilt edge and pulling it up to the snout, only the eyes\n     above it;\n  3. 🔴 sitting bolt upright from lying, chest lifted, both foretrotters planted, back straight,\n     head up, mouth wide;\n  4. flat on the belly, forelegs stretched forward, hind legs stretched back, body long and low,\n     one ear pressed flat to a vertical surface;\n  5. 🔴 flat on the belly, both foretrotters gripping a cloth and hauling it sideways, body\n     leaning back, one hind foot pushing the floor.\n\nSILHOUETTE\nA low round mass with two floppy ear triangles, and a straight back line when he sits up - never\nconfusable with the wolf's long-legged pointed-eared shape.\n\nREFERENCE SHEET\nOne flat dark field, figures floating, no ground, no scenery, no shadow, no lettering: full body\nside / three-quarter front / the five-pose strip / the four head states.\nSCENE token: PigletIn.",
   "PARTS SHEET - DoorKit   (bake this THIRD, attach as @image3 - this sheet is the book's machine)\n\nMedium: STYLE ANCHOR changjak-twobeams - flat opaque plates on a dark board, hard cut edges,\n0 shading. 🔴 BAKE THIS OR THE DOOR WILL DRIFT. One door decides fourteen pages, and p1 and p14\nmust be the same door down to the knots.\n\nDRAW THESE PARTS on a plain dark field, each separately, no scene, no ground:\n  1. 🔴 THE DOOR FROM OUTSIDE: five dark vertical planks of unequal width in bare board #151A20,\n     ONE pale birch board in reserve #F2EFE6 laid horizontally across the very bottom, a low flat\n     stone sill under it, a thick flat snow lump along the eave. Grain = at most 4 seam cuts in\n     total. Knots = exactly 2, one beside head height. 0 handle, 0 hinge plate, 0 keyhole, 0 sign.\n  2. 🔴 THE SAME DOOR FROM INSIDE: the same five planks and the same pale board at the foot, at\n     the SAME widths and in the SAME left-to-right order. Above it a thick wooden bar in two\n     brackets, set HIGH - about twice a piglet's standing height.\n  3. THE GAP UNDER THE DOOR at four widths, four separate strips: hair-thin / as wide as a paw\n     pad / a finger wide / a finger wide with a paw laid across it.\n  4. THE GREY RAG: one flat bare-board shape with a folded edge, shown twice - packed tight in\n     the gap, and half hauled out and rippling.\n  5. THE STRAW MAT AND QUILT: one flat low mat, one heavy quilt with 3 fold lines, no pattern.\n  6. BIRCH TRUNKS: three uprights in reserve #F2EFE6, each with at most 6 flat black bars, 0 twigs.\n  7. THE SNOW GROUND: one flat reserve field with a run of at most 8 paw marks cut into it.\n🔴 Lay parts 1 and 2 side by side on the sheet so the pale board can be checked: same width, same\nbrightness in both.\nNO letters, numbers or symbols anywhere on this sheet.\nSCENE token: DoorKit."
