   "CHARACTER SHEET - StonePieces   (bake FIRST, before anything else - this book is these pieces)\n\nThe stones as cut paper, no animals, laid on the plain GROUND #BFCBD0 sheet. 🔴 CUT ONE TRAPEZOID\nAND USE IT FOR EVERY STONE: wide at the top, narrow at the bottom, the same proportion every time.\n🔴 NO PIECE OVERLAPS ANOTHER ANYWHERE ON THIS SHEET.\n\n1 🔴 THE TEMPLATE: the trapezoid alone, in STONE #B7A98C laid paper with a visible grain and 0\n  marks inside it. Beside it the same shape in STONE INNER #93866C, one step darker - 🔴 this is\n  the UNDERSIDE face of a stone, not a shadow of one.\n2 🔴 TWO STONES MEETING: the same shape twice, set side by side so their slanted edges touch and\n  the sheet shows between them as ONE HAIRLINE. Then the same two pulled a little apart, so the\n  hairline becomes a band. 🔴 Same thing, two widths - fix that here, it is the whole book.\n3 🔴 THE ARCH PLATE, three states at one size:\n  A · two rows of trapezoids climbing from both banks to equal height, 🔴 AND A WIDE OPEN GAP AT\n    THE VERY TOP WHERE NO PIECE HAS BEEN LAID - the gap is simply GROUND, the same sheet as the\n    sky above it, and it is much wider than any hairline.\n  B · the same with 🔴 ONE WHITE PIECE SET INTO THAT GAP so its slanted sides meet its neighbours\n    and 🔴 THE WIDE GAP HAS BECOME A HAIRLINE. Nothing else changes between A and B.\n  C · the arch seen 🔴 FROM UNDERNEATH: the narrow bottom faces of the stones laid out as a FAN in\n    STONE INNER, hairlines radiating between them, and one wide GROUND gap at the crown. Count at\n    most 16 stones.\n4 🔴 THE WHITE STONE, large: the same trapezoid cut from #F6F2E6 paper with 🔴 NO GRAIN AND NO\n  PATTERN AT ALL - the only such piece in the book - carrying 🔴 ONE SMALL SHELL-SHAPED PUNCH MARK\n  and nothing else. Set it beside a STONE piece so the difference reads as GRAIN vs NO GRAIN, not\n  as light vs dark.\n5 THE FAILED SEATS, three small plates: the white piece resting on the SLANTED BACK of a stone with\n  only its lower corner touching · the same piece part way down that slope with 🔴 A NEW GAP OPEN\n  WHERE IT WAS · the piece lying flat on a plank with 🔴 A WIDE BAND OF GROUND ABOVE IT reaching up\n  to the stones. Stone dust = at most 6 tiny cut flecks.\n0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: StonePieces.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - OtterKit   (bake SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nCut paper, same make as the stones - flat pieces with scissor edges, no outline, no shading, no\nsheen, set edge to edge. It must look made of the same material as everything else.\n\nBODY: a young otter in dark brown #4B3A2C, the body one long flat piece with a thick tapering tail\n  cut as its own piece, short limbs as separate strips, a flat nose and three fine cut whiskers a\n  side. 🔴 THE SHORT LINEN WAISTCOAT #C9BFA4 IS ONE CUT PIECE laid against the chest with two front\n  edges - 🔴 IT IS PAPER, NOT CLOTH: no weave, no fibres, no stitching, no fraying. Wet fur = the\n  outline cut into a few soft points, never drawn strands.\nEYES: two small dark cut discs, no eyebrow, no expression, no catchlight. 🔴 The acting is the\n  spread of the legs, the line of the back and the height of the tail.\n🔴 THE POSTURE LADDER IS THE ACTING - six at one size in a row: SHOVING (bent right over a\n  wheelbarrow's handles, back curved, hind feet slipping on gravel) · HUGGING (a stone held under\n  both arms against the belly, upper body tipped back by the weight, cheek pressed to it) · LYING\n  OUT (flat on the stomach on a plank, both arms stretched far ahead, tail straight out behind) ·\n  HAULING (squatting in shallow water seen from behind, both hands on a corner and pulling toward\n  the body, hips back) · SAT DOWN HARD (legs apart in the water, one arm still holding a stone,\n  water thrown up as at most 6 small cut shapes) · 🔴 FLAT ON THE BACK, arms loose at the sides,\n  tail laid out, so that only a forehead, a nose tip and one wide eye would show at a frame edge.\n  Plus: STANDING UP TALL, one arm thrown straight up, heels lifted, tail bolt upright.\n🔴 THE TWO FOREPAWS, LARGE: both palms cupped as if still round a stone but 🔴 STOPPED A FINGER'S\n  WIDTH AWAY FROM IT, and one hand with three digits pushed into an empty space.\nPlain ground, no scenery. 0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: OtterKit.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - LynxMason   (bake THIRD, attach as @image3)\n\nCut paper, same make as OtterKit - attach the approved OtterKit sheet so the two are made by one\nhand and the size between them is settled. Plain GROUND. Colour: LYNX #9B8A72.\n\nBUILD: an old lynx, tall and square: heavy shoulders, a short blunt tail cut as its own piece, ear\n  tufts as two small pointed pieces, cheek ruff cut into the outline. 🔴 AT THUMBNAIL SIZE HE IS A\n  TALL SQUARE UPRIGHT AND THE OTTER IS A SMALL LOW CURVE - never confuse them. Spots = at most 9\n  small cut discs on the body, never a pattern paper and never painted marks. A tool belt is ONE\n  cut strip with a wooden maul hanging from it - 🔴 paper, not leather: no stitching, no tooling.\nFACE: two small dark cut discs, a level mouth. 🔴 HIS FACE DOES NOT CHANGE FOR THIRTEEN PAGES. He\n  is not stern, not amused and not encouraging; he watches a child put a stone in the wrong place\n  and 🔴 SAYS NOTHING AND DOES NOT REACH OUT. On ONE page only, one eyebrow piece sits a little\n  higher than the other - that is his entire range, and he does not smile with it.\n🔴 THE WORKING LADDER, four at one size: LIFTING (a stone held at chest height in both hands, arms\n  straight down with the weight, eyes on the one place it will meet) · SETTING (both hands easing a\n  stone sideways against its neighbour, the meeting edge dead centre) · 🔴 STANDING BY (arms\n  hanging, weight on one leg, looking down at something going wrong and not moving) · CROUCHED LOW\n  (knees bent, both hands on a maul haft, striking a wedge sideways from below).\n🔴 HIS TWO HANDS, LARGE: one hand under a stone and the other easing its side edge flush against\n  the next one. 🔴 He has no pose in which he points, beckons, warns, demonstrates or takes the\n  work out of the child's hands.\nPlain ground, no scenery. 0 lettering, 0 numerals. SCENE token: LynxMason.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - CreekSet   (bake FOURTH, attach as @image4)\n\nThe place, empty of animals, in cut paper on the GROUND #BFCBD0 sheet, no piece overlapping any\nother.\n\nTHE PLACE, fixed for fourteen pages: a shallow creek running across the frame in WATER #4E9B8E with\n  a SHALLOWS #77B3A5 piece where the bed is close, gravel banks either side, one low ridge behind\n  as a single cut piece, and a wheelbarrow on the near bank. 🔴 Draw the whole crossing once, wide,\n  from the far bank.\n🔴 THE FALSEWORK, three states at one size - this is the clock of the book:\n  A · STANDING: a half-moon timber frame #6F5638 in the water on four posts with wedges at their\n    feet, PLANK #8A6E48 boards laid over it, 🔴 ITS CURVED TOP EDGE TOUCHING THE UNDERSIDE OF THE\n    STONES ALL ALONG - no gap between timber and stone anywhere.\n  B · SETTLED: the same frame dropped a handspan, so 🔴 ONE THIN BAND OF GROUND NOW RUNS BETWEEN\n    THE STONES AND THE TIMBER along the whole curve. One wedge is out and floating away.\n  C · 🔴 GONE: no frame, no posts, no wedges - under the arch there is only GROUND, the far bank\n    and the water. Two loose logs lie on the gravel.\n  🔴 Set A and C side by side: one is packed with timber and one is empty, and that is the landing.\n🔴 THE EVENING SET: the same STONE, TIMBER, WATER and LYNX papers cut again 🔴 ONE STEP DARKER, so\n  a whole scene can be swapped at once. 🔴 THE WHITE STONE IS NOT REMADE - it stays #F6F2E6, so in\n  the evening set it is the only piece that has not gone down a step.\nAlso: the wheelbarrow with five or six stone pieces in it, and the same barrow empty; loose wedges;\n  a wooden maul. 0 lettering, 0 numerals, no signs. SCENE token: CreekSet."
