   "CHARACTER SHEET - DonkeyOld   (bake this FIRST, attach as @image1)\n\nMedium: STYLE ANCHOR changjak-finishshift - opaque gouache on warm laid paper, visible brush\nmarks, flat light, no rendering. He is never the most finished thing on a page.\n\nFACE: an old donkey. Long head, heavy muzzle. 🔴 THE FRONT TEETH ARE GONE - when the mouth opens\nthe gap shows as one flat dark shape, and it is the same gap every page. Hollow cheeks, wide open\nnostrils, one small round dark dot per eye, no whites, no eyebrows. Ears long: draw them BACK AND\nFLAT for failure and 🔴 STRAIGHT UP AND FORWARD once, for p12 only.\nCOAT: painted in clumps, each 3-5 strokes, at most 12 clumps on the whole animal. Mane sparse and\nbroken, tail thin with a short tuft. Ribs and spine showing as at most 4 strokes.\nBUILD: tall and bony, big head, long neck, knees thick. He never stands upright and never grips:\nhe braces on four legs, and steadies a pipe with a shoulder or with one lifted forehoof.\nCLOTHES: none, at any point in the book.\nDISTINGUISHING: the LONGEST NECK AND LARGEST HEAD of the four - he alone can lower his mouth to a\nfloor-level pipe mouth without lying down.\nREFERENCE SHEET: 5 views on bare warm paper, evenly spaced, no ground line -\n(1) standing four-square, side view, head low;\n(2) 🔴 forelegs braced apart, NECK STRETCHED STRAIGHT UP, mouth open, cheeks hollow (posture 1);\n(3) 🔴 head hanging right down, eyes on his own hoofs, lips loose and open (posture 2 - the page\n    where he cannot see what his own breath is doing);\n(4) face pushed close to a wall, mouth open against something at head height (posture 3);\n(5) 🔴 neck bent down, mouth laid whole over a wide opening at knee height, one forehoof against\n    a shaft, ribs drawn in, EARS UP (posture 4).\nNO letters, numbers or symbols anywhere on the sheet.\nSCENE token: DonkeyOld.",
   "CHARACTER SHEET - DogOld   (bake this SECOND, attach as @image2)\n\nMedium: STYLE ANCHOR changjak-finishshift - opaque gouache on warm laid paper, visible brush\nmarks, flat light, no rendering.\n\nFACE: an old dog, broad head, heavy muzzle gone white round the mouth, eyelids sagging, one small\nround dark dot per eye, no whites, no eyebrows. 🔴 WHEN HE OPENS HIS MOUTH THE TONGUE HANGS SLACK\nAND THE THROAT DOES NOT WORK - the open mouth is a soft loose shape, never a bark shape.\nCOAT: clumps of 3-5 strokes, at most 12 on the whole animal, thin over the hips. A few loose hairs\nwhere he has been lying, at most 5.\nBUILD: heavy chest, low back, thick legs, stiff hind quarters. He rises front-first: forelegs\nstraighten while the hind legs stay folded. Ribs swell and fall with the breath - that swell is\nhis one piece of acting.\nCLOTHES: none.\nDISTINGUISHING: the LOW HEAVY MASS at floor level - beside the donkey's height and the cat's line\nhe is the wide shape. He is the only one who works from under the bench.\nREFERENCE SHEET: 4 views on bare warm paper, evenly spaced, no ground line -\n(1) lying with the chin on the forepaws, only the eyes lifted (posture 1 - his idle place);\n(2) 🔴 front half risen, forelegs straight, hind legs still folded, neck out, mouth open, ribs\n    swollen (posture 2);\n(3) 🔴 hind legs bracing, both forepaws up on a high flat surface, neck stretched long, nose and\n    mouth pressed to something under it, eyes shut, back rounded (posture 3);\n(4) sitting up with a thick pipe held upright between the forelegs, chest swollen (posture 4).\nNO letters, numbers or symbols anywhere on the sheet.\nSCENE token: DogOld.",
